th='Value every sense – use your eyes as if tomorrow you would be stricken blind.',
sum='Helen Keller, who could neither see nor hear, tells how much pleasure she gets through mere touch – the delicate symmetry of a leaf, the smooth skin of a silver birch, the rough, shaggy bark of a pine, a bud in spring, and the happy quiver of a bird in full song. She imagines what she would like to see if she were given sight for just three days. On the first day she would see the people whose kindness and companionship have made her life worth living, and look into their eyes – the “window of the soul”. On the second day she would rise with the dawn to see night turn into day, and then visit the museums to see the pageant of man’s progress – the history of the earth, dinosaurs and mastodons. On the third day she would greet the dawn again and go to the city, stand at a busy corner and watch people in their daily lives – their smiles, determination and suffering. At midnight permanent night would close in again. Her advice to those who can see: use your eyes as if tomorrow you would be stricken blind; hear, touch, smell and taste in the same way. Make the most of every sense – but sight must be the most delightful of all.',
